{"entries":{"chapter-34-study-hearing-loss-assessment-and-management|Chapter 34 Study: Hearing Loss: Assessment and Management":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Presbycusis is heterogeneous age-related peripheral and central auditory dysfunction; it usually begins at high frequencies and progresses into the speech range (p489–490).":["u3"],"copy|Significant loss affects approximately 44% in their sixties, 66% at 70–79, and 90% after 80 in the chapter's cited estimates (p490).":["u4"],"copy|Genetic susceptibility interacts with noise, ototoxic drugs, smoking and comorbidity; diabetes and vascular disease can promote cochlear injury (p489–491).":["u5"],"copy|Ask about speech understanding, especially in noise, rather than only whether sounds are audible. A hearing-handicap questionnaire can identify everyday effects (p489, p493, p500).":["u6"],"copy|Pure-tone audiometry, tympanometry, acoustic reflexes and word-recognition testing characterize different aspects of impairment (p489).":["u7"],"copy|One cited professional definition uses threshold loss of at least 25 dB at any frequency between 250 and 8000 Hz; functional impact also depends on frequency pattern (p493).":["u8"],"copy|Sensorineural loss often begins with high-frequency hair-cell damage; a flat audiogram can reflect metabolic/supporting-structure dysfunction (p495).":["u9"],"copy|Tailor digital hearing aids to the audiogram; they can improve hearing and speech discrimination but do not regenerate lost hair cells (p499).":["u10"],"copy|Address acceptance and continued use, not just device provision; many eligible older people never try an aid or stop using it (p499).":["u11"],"copy|When aids cannot provide sufficient correction, consider cochlear implantation. The chapter cites benefit when sentence scores in the poorer ear are below 60% (p499).":["u12"],"copy|Hearing aids amplify sound using remaining sensory cells; implants bypass those cells to stimulate the auditory nerve electrically (p499).":["u13"],"copy|Reduce noise, ototoxin and smoking exposure; exercise and healthy diet may help, but the degree to which specific interventions delay presbycusis remains uncertain (p498).":["u14"],"copy|Antioxidant results are inconsistent; a small low-frequency benefit from folate in a Dutch study cannot be generalized to restoration of high-frequency hearing (p499).":["u15"],"copy|“I hear but cannot understand” may reflect central auditory dysfunction that amplification alone cannot resolve (p499–500).":["u16"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u17"],"copy|2025-Jun Q26":["u18"],"copy|CHAPTER 34s / HAZZARD":["u19"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 34 - Hearing Loss: Assessment and Management; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u20"],"copy|Excluded from the IMA required reading list (P005-2026); cited once in past papers.":["u21"],"copy|Definition & epidemiology":["u22"],"copy|Assessment":["u23"],"copy|Management":["u24"],"copy|Prevention, drugs & exam traps":["u25"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (1):":["u26"],"copy|P005-2026 excludes Chapter 34 and separately assigns the NEJM review on age-related hearing loss.":["u21"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (3):":["u26"]},"maxId":26}
